---
import type { Props } from "../components/Head.astro";
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import LeafShadowSimulator from "../components/LeafShadowSimulator.tsx";

const layoutProps: Props = {
  title: "Leaf Shadows",
  description:
    "An interactive simulation of dappled sunlight under a tree — and why the spots of light are round.",
  path: "/leaf-shadow",
};
---

<Layout {...layoutProps} className="prose prose-stone mx-auto max-w-2xl">
  <h1>Leaf shadows</h1>
  <p>
    On a sunny day, the light under a tree isn't shaped like leaves — it's
    scattered with little <em>round</em> spots. Each gap between the leaves is
    small enough to act like a pinhole camera, projecting an upside-down image
    of the sun onto the ground. Since the sun is a disc, the spots are circles.
  </p>

  <LeafShadowSimulator client:only="solid-js" />

  <p>
    The simulation convolves a procedural canopy with the shape of the sun:
    tiny gaps reproduce the sun's disc exactly (the circular spots), while wide
    openings just let through soft-edged patches. Drag <strong>Eclipse</strong>
    to shrink the sun into a crescent — during a partial solar eclipse the
    ground fills with crescents instead of circles, the same camera-obscura
    effect at work.
  </p>
  <p class="text-sm text-stone-500">
    Tip: raise <strong>Sun-spot size</strong> to lift the canopy higher (bigger,
    softer spots), and lower <strong>Gap size</strong> for a denser canopy with
    sharper pinholes.
  </p>
</Layout>
